still reeling from the first wave of  coronavirus  care homes fear the elderly are being  sacrificed once more  sky news can reveal plans to set up  covet units within existing care homes  which multiple providers have described  as reckless  and an attempt to keep care home  residents out of hospitals  we've obtained a copy of an email sent  to care homes in county durham  it says we are inviting expressions of  interest from care homes who are able to  offer block  booked beds for service users who test  positive for covid19  over the winter period this will apply  to both service users who are discharged  from hospital  as well as potentially service users  within the community  who require 24-hour care to avoid  hospital admittance we're also now  working with cqc  on actually actively inspecting and  designating  specific facilities the care minister  helen waitley giving evidence to a  common select committee  said specific facilities would be set up  for covered positive patients  but care england says it believes  throughout the country  only a handful of care homes will be  able to set up  entirely separate units prompting  serious concerns about the spread of the  virus  it was completely ridiculous for a  scheme to be announced at the center  and then for it to be left to the  vagaries of 300 nod local authorities  the bottom line is that we are in the  middle of a major health emergency and  what we need is strong direction  and clear leadership from the center the  government says its  absolute priority is to prevent  infections in care homes  but providers know only too well of the  dangers of the disease  in county durham providers have been  told the dedicated unit should be  self-contained with access as separate  as possible  it is also preferable if where feasible  dedicated kitchen areas and bathroom  facilities can be provided  but care homes we've spoken to say the  separation of all  covered and non-covered facilities  should be mandatory  i'm worried about what they're going to  bend to get the ends they want  we can't mix populations we can't put  our current residents at risk  unless the unit is physically removed  from the original or existing building  if it's a part of that same building i  don't think it makes a difference  because the virus is too clever and too  good at getting around  there should be a separate facility a  so-called hot home  which is just for the care of the kovid  positive think of it  as a nightingale nursing home paul  sexton says he wants to speak  out on behalf of those with loved ones  in care homes  his mother kathleen was 84 when she died  of coronavirus  in a care home in county durham in may  angry about government guidelines at the  start of the pandemic which saw  untested coveted patients discharged to  care homes  he believes the winter strategy is  flawed  it just doesn't stack up why would you  put a virus in a care home  we have two is coming well it's here and  we're doing exactly the same  as a way of warning we know we can't  contain the virus  and are we are people going to die  necessarily again it is chilling it  really is chilly  providers are asking why facilities like  the nightingale  hospitals can't be used to help care for  the elderly  the department of health and social care  says it's working with the care quality  commission  to ensure care homes which take covered  positive patients  can provide safe care lisa holland  sky news
 

